gmJ(){return"A CrossFit classic since 2004. Five pull-ups, ten push-ups, fifteen squats \u2014 as many rounds as you can in twenty minutes. Tom Holland calls it his go-to session: you don't have to think about anything, you just count your rounds. His record is 27 rounds."},
gmI(){return"The same round as Cindy, except now it has to fit inside a minute. Finish in forty seconds and the rest is yours; miss it and the round is gone \u2014 the next one starts anyway."},
gmG(){return"Four hundred reps, one block at a time. No switching between movements, no hiding: you finish all hundred pull-ups before the first push-up."},
gmH(){return"Two movements, a descending ladder and nowhere to rest. Fifteen minutes is a generous cap \u2014 most of the work is in the first two rounds."},
gmT(){return"Cindy's harder sister: handstand push-ups, pistols and pull-ups. Five rounds here is already a level most people never reach."},
gmU(){return"Run four hundred metres, then hang on the bar for as long as you can. Only the pull-ups count \u2014 the run is the rest."},
gmV(){return"Twenty seconds on, ten off, eight sets \u2014 four minutes. In 1996 Japanese physiologist Izumi Tabata measured this format on a cycle ergometer and showed it taxes the aerobic and anaerobic systems at once. The structure is the easy part: by the seventh set there should be nothing left."},
gmK(){return"The workout that ends itself. Every minute you owe one more burpee than the last, and sooner or later the minute runs out before you do."},
gmN(){return"Twenty-one, fifteen, nine: thrusters and pull-ups. The most famous way to find your ceiling. Under nine minutes is a solid time; the best go under three."},
gmP(){return"Three rounds of running, swings and pull-ups \u2014 the benchmark for mixed conditioning. Pacing the first run decides the whole thing."},
gmO(){return"Thirty clean and jerks, nothing else. Pure barbell cycling: technique goes first, then the grip, then the plan."},
gmS(){return"One hundred and fifty wall-ball shots. Simple to describe, brutal to hold \u2014 the squat is what fails, not the arms."},
gmL(){return"Deadlifts and handstand push-ups, twenty-one, fifteen, nine. Heavy pulling against being upside down \u2014 a strange, very honest combination."},
gmQ(){return"Thirty snatches for time. Single-movement benchmarks leave no room to hide behind pacing."},
gmR(){return"Row, thrusters, pull-ups \u2014 three stations, no rest. The thrusters are light on purpose: the test is whether you can keep going after the row."},
gmM(){return"Three rounds of five stations, a minute each, named after a fighter's description of how it feels. Seventeen minutes total, and the score is every rep from every station added up."},
